@c *********************************
@node About This Guide
@unnumbered About This Guide

@noindent
This guide contains a general description of @value{gnatstack}, a
framework for computing and analyzing stack usage for the different
subprograms in the partition and extracting worst case prior to
execution time.

@c *********************************
@node The GNATstack Tool
@chapter The GNATstack Tool

@noindent
The @value{gnatstack} tool statically predicts the maximum stack space
required by every task in an application. The computed bounds can be  
used to ensure that sufficient space is reserved, thus guaranteeing  
safe execution with respect to stack usage. The tool uses a  
conservative analysis to deal with complexities such as subprogram  
recursion, while avoiding unnecessarily pessimistic estimates.

This static stack analysis tool exploits data generated by the  
compiler to compute worst-case stack requirements. It perform
per-subprogram stack consumption computation combined with control
flow analysis.

This is a static tool in the sense that its computation is based on  
information known at compile time. It implies that when the tool  
indicates that the result is accurate then the computed bound can  
never overflow. The tool does not rely on empiric run-time
information.

On the other hand, there may be situations in which the results are
not accurate (the tool will actually indicate this situation) because
of some missing information (such as subprogram recursion, indirect
calls, etc.). The infrastructure to allow users to specify this
missing information is provided by the tool.

The main output of the tool is the worst-case stack requirements for  
every entry point, together with the paths that lead to these stack  
needs. The list of entry points can be automatically computed (all  
the tasks, including the environment task) or can be specified by the  
user (a list of entry points or all the subprograms matching a  
certain regular expression).

@value{gnatstack} can also detect and display a list of potential
problems when computing stack requirements:

@itemize @bullet
@item
 Indirect (including dispatching) calls. The tool indicates the number
 of indirect calls made from any subprogram.
@cindex Indirect calls
@cindex Dispatching calls

@item
External calls. The tool displays all the subprograms that are  
reachable from any entry point for which we do not have any stack or  
call graph information.
@cindex External calls

@item
Unbounded frames. The tool displays all the subprograms that are  
reachable from any entry point with an unbounded stack requirements.  
The required stack size depends on the arguments passed to the  
subprogram.
@cindex Unbounded frames

@item
Cycles. The tool can detect all the cycles in the call graph. These
cycles represent potential recursion and hence potentially unbounded
stack consumption.
@cindex Cycles
@end itemize

@noindent
@value{gnatstack} allows the user to specify in a text file the missing  
information, such as the potential targets for indirect calls, stack  
requirements for externals calls, and user-defined bounds for  
unbounded frames.

@c *********************************
@node Getting Started with GNATstack
@chapter Getting Started with GNATstack

@noindent
Two steps are needed to analyze stack usage: generation of the basic
stack consumption and callgraph information and worst-case stack
analysis.

The first step is to compile the application with the required options
to generate the basic information about per-subprogram stack
consumption and callgraph information. The
@option{-fcallgraph-info=su} compiler switch outputs this information
on a per-file basis in the common VCG format.
@cindex @code{-fcallgraph-info=su}

@smallexample
$ gnatmake Main_Unit -cargs -fcallgraph-info=su
@end smallexample

@noindent
The use of this compiler switch generates a @file{.ci} file for every
compilation unit.

The @value{gnatstack} can then parse these files to generate the
complete call graph decorated with stack usage information, so it can
look for the longest paths in terms of stack usage.

@smallexample
$ gnatstack *.ci
Worst case analysis is *not* accurate ; use -W options for details

Accumulated stack usage information for entry points

main : total 192 bytes
@end smallexample

