@node Options for GNATstack
@section Options for GNATstack

@noindent
@value{gnatstack} is very configurable with respect to the amount of
information that we want to obtain. The set of options available is
the following:

@table @option
@c !sort!
@item ^-a^/ALL_SUBPROGRAMS^
@cindex @option{^-a^/ALL_SUBPROGRAMS^}

Consider all subprograms as entry points.

@item ^-ca^/ALL_CYCLES^
@cindex @option{^-ca^/ALL_CYCLES^}
Extract all possible cycles in the call graph.

@item ^-g^/DEBUG^
@cindex @option{^-g^/DEBUG^}
Generate internal debug information.

@item ^-aO^/DIRECTORY=^@var{dir}
@cindex @option{^-aO^/DIRECTORY^}
Load @file{.ci} files from @var{dir}.

@item ^-e^/ENTRIES=^@var{entry_point[,...]}
@cindex @option{^-e^/ENTRIES^}
Name of symbols to be used as entry points for the analysis.

@item ^-files^/FILES^=@var{file}
@cindex @option{^-files^/FILES^}
Take as arguments the files listed in text file @var{file}.
Text file @var{file} may contain empty lines that are ignored.
Each non empty line should contain the name of an existing file.
Several such switches may be specified simultaneously.

@item ^-h^/HELP^
@cindex @option{^h^/HELP^}
Output a message explaining the usage of @command{gnatstack}.

@item ^-l^/LIST=^@var{n}
@cindex @option{^-l^/LIST^}
Print the @var{n} subprograms requiring the biggest local stack
usage. By default none will be displayed.

@item ^-o^/ORDER=^@var{x}
@cindex @option{^-o^/ORDER^}
Specifies the order for displaying the different call graphs.
The options allowed for this qualifier are:

@table @option
@c !sort!

@item ^-os^/ORDER=STACK^
@cindex @option{^-os^/ORDER=STACK^}
Select stack usage order (the default mode).

@item ^-oa^/ORDER=ALPHABETICAL^
@cindex @option{^-oa^/ORDER=ALPHABETICAL^}
Select alphabetical order.

@end table

@item ^-p^/PATH^
@cindex @option{^-p^/PATH^}
Print all the subprograms that make up the worst-case path for every
entry point.

@item ^-f^OUTPUT=^@var{file}
@cindex @option{^-f^OUTPUT^}
Write the generated graph (VCG format) into @var{file}.

@item ^-r^/EXPRESSION=^@var{regexp}
@cindex @option{^-r^/EXPRESSION^}
Any symbol matching the regular expression @var{regexp} will be
considered as an entry point for the analysis.

@item ^-d^/UNBOUNDED=^@var{n}
@cindex @option{^-d^/UNBOUNDED^}
Set default stack size for unbounded (dynamic) frames to @var{n} bytes. 

@item ^-u^/UNKNOWN=^@var{n}
@cindex @option{^-u^/UNKNOWN^}
Set default stack size for unknown (external) calls to @var{n} bytes.

@item ^-v^/VERBOSE^
@cindex @option{^-v^/VERBOSE^}
Specify the amount of information to be displayed about the
different subprograms. In verbose mode the full location of the
subprogram will be part of the output, as well as detailed information
about inaccurate data.

@item ^-W^/WARNINGS=^@var{x}
@cindex @option{^-W^/WARNINGS^}
Warning mode. The options allowed for this qualifier are:

@table @option
@c !sort!

@item ^-Wa^/WARNINGS=ALL^
@cindex @option{^-Wa^/WARNINGS=ALL^}
Turn on all optional warnings.

@item ^-Wc^/WARNINGS=CYCLES^
@cindex @option{^-Wc^/WARNINGS=CYCLES^}
Turn on warnings for cycles (recursion).

@item ^-Wu^/WARNINGS=UNBOUNDED^
@cindex @option{^-Wu^/WARNINGS=UNBOUNDED^}
Turn on warnings for unbounded frames.

@item ^-We^/WARNINGS=EXTERNAL^
@cindex @option{^-We^/WARNINGS=EXTERNAL^}
Turn on warnings for external calls.

@item ^-Wi^/WARNINGS=INDIRECT^
@cindex @option{^-Wi^/WARNINGS=INDIRECT^}
Turn on warnings for indirect (including dispatching) calls.

@end table

@end table

@node Use of Project Files
@section  Use of Project Files

@noindent
@command{gnatstack} can be invoked through the @command{gnat} driver
(see @cite{GNAT User's Guide}) and hence take advantage of the support
for project files. The list of project-related switches that are
available are:

@table @option

@item ^-P^/PROJECT_FILE=^@var{project}
@cindex @option{^-P^/PROJECT_FILE=^}
Indicates the name of a project file. This project file will be parsed with
the verbosity indicated by @option{^-vP^MESSAGE_PROJECT_FILES=^@emph{x}},
if any, and using the external references indicated
by @option{^-X^/EXTERNAL_REFERENCE^} switches, if any.
@ifclear vms
There may zero, one or more spaces between @option{-P} and @var{project}.
@end ifclear

@noindent
There must be only one @option{^-P^/PROJECT_FILE^} switch on the command line.

@noindent
Since the Project Manager parses the project file only after all the switches
on the command line are checked, the order of the switches
@option{^-P^/PROJECT_FILE^},
@option{^-vP^/MESSAGES_PROJECT_FILE=^@emph{x}}
or @option{^-X^/EXTERNAL_REFERENCE^} is not significant.

@item ^-X^/EXTERNAL_REFERENCE=^@var{name=value}
@cindex @option{^-X^/EXTERNAL_REFERENCE^}
Indicates that external variable @var{name} has the value @var{value}.
The Project Manager will use this value for occurrences of
@code{external(name)} when parsing the project file.

@ifclear vms
@noindent
If @var{name} or @var{value} includes a space, then @var{name=value} should be
put between quotes.
@smallexample
  -XOS=NT
  -X"user=John Doe"
@end smallexample
@end ifclear

@noindent
Several @option{^-X^/EXTERNAL_REFERENCE^} switches can be used simultaneously.
If several @option{^-X^/EXTERNAL_REFERENCE^} switches specify the same
@var{name}, only the last one is used.

@noindent
An external variable specified with a @option{^-X^/EXTERNAL_REFERENCE^} switch
takes precedence over the value of the same name in the environment.

@item ^-U^/ALL_PROJECTS^
@cindex @option{^-U^/ALL_PROJECTS^}
When used with a project file and no @file{.ci} files on the command
line, the @file{.ci} files for all sources of all project files are
passed when invoking @command{gnatstack}.

@item ^-vP^/MESSAGES_PROJECT_FILE=^@emph{x}
@cindex @option{^-vP^/MESSAGES_PROJECT_FILE^}
Indicates the verbosity of the parsing of GNAT project files.

@ifclear vms
@option{-vP0} means Default;
@option{-vP1} means Medium;
@option{-vP2} means High.
@end ifclear

@ifset vms
There are three possible options for this qualifier: DEFAULT, MEDIUM and
HIGH.
@end ifset

@noindent
The default is @option{^Default^DEFAULT^}: no output for syntactically
correct project files.
@noindent
If several @option{^-vP^/MESSAGES_PROJECT_FILE=^@emph{x}} switches are present,
only the last one is used.

@end table

@noindent
There is an optional package in the project file for @value{gnatstack}
(which is the package @code{Stack}) that has an unique attribute
@code{Switches}. This attribute is a simple variable that contains a
string list value representing the switches to be passed to
@command{gnatstack}.

@smallexample @c projectfile
@group
project Prj is
   package Stack is
      for Switches use ("-p");
   end Stack;
end Prj;
@end group
@end smallexample

@noindent
The following command will parse the project file @file{prj.gpr} and
call @command{gnatstack} with the specified options. 

@smallexample
$ gnat stack -P prj.gpr
@end smallexample

@noindent
When @command{gnat stack} is used with a project file, but with no source
specified on the command line, it passes to the @command{gnatstack}
tool the set of @file{.ci} files that correspond to the units that
belong to the project. When adding the @option{^-U^/ALL_PROJECTS^}
option all projects in the project tree will be taken into account.


@c *********************************
@node Limitations
@chapter Limitations

@noindent
As indicated in @ref{The GNATstack Tool}, there is a list of
potential problems when computing stack requirements: indirect and
external calls, unbounded frames, and recursion. This chapter provides
guidance about how to address these issues.

The first possibility to address these issues is to avoid the
ocurrence of these events by means of using the appropriate
restrictions and coding style rules. The second is to supply
additional information that will help @value{gnatstack} to proceed the
analysis.

@itemize @bullet
@item Indirect calls.
@cindex Indirect calls
@cindex Dispatching calls
Determining statically the target subprogram when dereferencing an
access-to-subprogram variable is not possible. These occurrences can
be forbidden through @code{pragma Restrictions}. The use of
@code{No_Access_Subprograms} avoids access-to-subprogram
types. Dispatching calls can also be restricted by using
@code{No_Dispatch} (which prohibits classwide constructs), or the less
restricting @code{No_Dispatching_Calls} (which ensures that no
dispatching calls are present).
@cindex @code{pragma Restrictions}
@cindex @code{No_Access_Subprograms} restriction
@cindex @code{No_Dispatch} restriction
@cindex @code{No_Dispatching_Calls} restriction
The @option{^-Wi^/WARNINGS=INDIRECT^} option can be used to signal all
the occurrences of indirect calls in the program under analysis.
@cindex @option{^-Wi^/WARNINGS=INDIRECT^}

@item External calls.
@cindex External calls
These are calls to subprograms for which no stack usage or call graph
information is available, introducing an unknown amount of stack usage
in a call chain. It can only be avoided by providing the required
information for the objects and libraries that make up the
application.
The @option{^-We^/WARNINGS=EXTERNAL^} option can be used to signal all
the occurrences of external calls in the program under analysis.
@cindex @option{^-We^/WARNINGS=EXTERNAL^}

@item Unbounded frame.
@cindex Unbounded frame
The use of dynamically sized local objects (such as those whose size
depends on an input argument) make it impossible to statically bound
stack usage.
The @option{^-Wu^/WARNINGS=UNBOUNDED^} option can be used to signal
all the occurrences of external calls in the program under analysis.
@cindex @option{^-Wu^/WARNINGS=UNBOUNDED^}

@item Recursion.
@cindex Recursion
Very simple cases can be detected by using @code{pragma Restrictions
(No_Recursion)}. This restriction ensures that as part of the
execution of a subprogram the same subprogram is not invoked. More
complex situations require call graph analysis. @command{gnatcheck},
see @cite{GNAT User's Guide}, can be used to detect potential
recursion. @command{gnatstack} can also detect all the occurrences of
cycles in the call graph using the options @option{^-ca
-Wc^/ALL_CYCLES /WARNINGS=CYCLES^}.
@cindex @code{pragma Restrictions}
@cindex @code{No_Recursion} restriction
@cindex @option{^-ca^/ALL_CYCLES^}
@cindex @option{^-Wc^/WARNINGS=CYCLES^}

@end itemize

@noindent
If the occurrence of these potential problem cannot be avoided,
@value{gnatstack} provides the means to supply additional stack usage
and call graph information.

When @value{gnatstack} finds incomplete information it generates a
file (@file{undefined.ciu}) containing signaling the missing
information. The format of this file is similar to the @file{.ci}
files.

When there is some missing information the result of the static stack
analysis is not accurate. This situation is indicated by
@value{gnatstack} when displaying the results with the following
warning message:

@smallexample
Worst case analysis is *not* accurate ; use -W options for details
@end smallexample

@noindent
@value{gnatstack} provides as well accuracy information for the
different subprograms. The mark @code{*} is attached to the
subprograms when there is any missing information.


@c *********************************
@node Examples
@chapter Examples

@noindent
This section contains a number of examples of using the
@value{gnatstack} tool showing how to use to tool and how to interpret
the results. Let us start analyzing a simple program like the
following:

@smallexample @c ada
@group
procedure Main_Unit is
   type Data_Type is array (1 .. 5) of Integer;

   function Inverse (Input : Data_Type) return Data_Type is
      Result : Data_Type;
   begin
      for Index in Data_Type'Range loop
         Result (Index) := Input (Data_Type'Last -
                                  (Index - Data_Type'First));
      end loop;

      return Result;
   end Inverse;

   Data   : Data_Type := (1, 2, 3, 4, 5);
   Result : Data_Type;
begin
   Result := Inverse (Data);
end Main_Unit;
@end group
@end smallexample

@noindent
A typical project file would contain the flags to compile the
application with the required options to generate stack consumption
and callgraph information. In addition, it would contain the desired
option for the stack analysis phase:

@smallexample @c projectfile
@group
project Prj is
   for Main use ("main_unit.adb");

   package Compiler is
      for Default_Switches ("Ada") use ("-fcallgraph-info=su");
   end Compiler;

   package Stack is
      for Switches use ("-p");
   end Stack;
end Prj;
@end group
@end smallexample

@noindent
We can then easily compile and analyze the application using this
project file:

@smallexample
$ gnat make -P prj.gpr
[...]
$ gnat stack -P prj.gpr
Worst case analysis is *not* accurate ; use -W options for details

Accumulated stack usage information for entry points

main : total 192 bytes
 +-> main
 +-> main_unit
 +-> main_unit.inverse
@end smallexample

@noindent
Note that we do not need to specify the @file{.ci} files for the
application. The @command{gnat} driver computes automatically this
list. In addition, the main entry point has also been automatically
detected by @command{gnatstack}.

The execution of the tool shows two basic things. First it tells that
the longest path (in terms of stack consumption) is that made up by
@code{main} (the program entry point) -> @code{main_unit} (the main
Ada program) -> @code{main_unit.inverse}. The maximum stack
consumption following this path is 192 bytes, and this bound can be
trusted. The second important thing is that the worst case analysis is
not accurate. As indicated in the warning message, we can check which
is the origin of this inaccuracy using the @option{^-W^/WARNINGS=^}
option. If we proceed as suggested:

@smallexample
$ gnat stack -P prj.gpr -Wa
Worst case analysis is *not* accurate ; use -W options for details

List of external subprograms:

  __gnat_break_start
  __gnat_finalize
  __gnat_initialize
  __gnat_install_handler
  memset
  system.exception_table._elabb
  system.secondary_stack._elabb
  system.soft_links._elabb
  system.standard_library.adafinal

Accumulated stack usage information for entry points

main : total 192 bytes
 +-> main
 +-> main_unit
 +-> main_unit.inverse
@end smallexample

@noindent
We can see tha the result is not accurate because there are symbols
(invoked during the initialization phase) for which there is no stack
usage information.

Additional information can be extracted increasing the verbosity
level. Adding the @option{^-v^/VERBOSE^} option will also print
detailed information about the location of the different subprograms
and per-subprogram stack requirements:

@smallexample
$ gnat stack -v -P prj.gpr
Worst case analysis is *not* accurate ; use -W options for details

Accumulated stack usage information for entry points

main : total 192 bytes
 +-> main at main:b~main_unit.ads:33:13 : 48 bytes
 +-> main_unit at ada_main_program:b~main_unit.adb:125:17,
     Main_Unit:main_unit.adb:1:11 : 64 bytes
 +-> main_unit.inverse at Inverse:main_unit.adb:4:13 : 80 bytes
@end smallexample

@noindent
Focussing on the capability of detecting problems related to the
computation of stack requirements we can use the following example:

@smallexample @c ada
@group
with Ada.Calendar;

procedure P is

   procedure Q is
      Size : Integer := Ada.Calendar.Year (Ada.Calendar.Clock);

      Data : array (1 .. Size) of Integer;
      --  Dynamically sized local objects

   begin
      P;
   end Q;
begin
   Q;
end P;
@end group
@end smallexample

@noindent
To compile and analyze this program we do:

@smallexample
$ gnatmake p -cargs -fcallgraph-info=su
[...]
$ gnatstack *.ci
Worst case analysis is *not* accurate ; use -W options for details

Accumulated stack usage information for entry points

main : total 128 bytes *
@end smallexample

@noindent
The result of the analysis is not accurate, and we can add different
options to better understand what is happening. For example, we can
request the path, warnings, and verbose information doing:

@smallexample
$ gnatstack -p -Wa -v *.ci
Worst case analysis is *not* accurate ; use -W options for details

List of cycles:

<c0> p.q
 +-> p.q at Q:p.adb:7:14 : 48 bytes (unbounded,cycle) (<c0>)
 +-> p.r at R:p.adb:5:14 : 16 bytes (cycle) (<c0>)
 +-> p.q at Q:p.adb:7:14 : 48 bytes (unbounded,cycle) (<c0>)


List of subprograms with dynamic unbounded frames:

  p.q at Q:p.adb:7:14

List of external subprograms:

  __gnat_break_start at break_start:b~p.ads:30:14
  __gnat_finalize at finalize:b~p.adb:126:17
  __gnat_initialize
  __gnat_install_handler at install_handler:b~p.adb:51:17
  ada.calendar._elabb at e42b:b~p.adb:108:19
  ada.calendar._elabs at e40b:b~p.adb:107:19
  ada.calendar.clock
  ada.calendar.year
  memset
  system.exception_table._elabb at e38b:b~p.adb:105:29
  system.secondary_stack._elabb at e36b:b~p.adb:103:29
  system.soft_links._elabb at e34b:b~p.adb:101:24
  system.standard_library.adafinal

Accumulated stack usage information for entry points

main : total 128 bytes (unbounded,cycle) *
 +-> main at main:b~p.ads:33:13 : 48 bytes
 +-> p at ada_main_program:b~p.adb:129:17,P:p.adb:3:11 : 16 bytes
 +-> p.q at Q:p.adb:7:14 : 48 bytes (unbounded,cycle) (<c0>)
 +-> p.r at R:p.adb:5:14 : 16 bytes (cycle) (<c0>)
@end smallexample

@noindent
@value{gnatstack} has detected one cycle in the call graph (@code{Q}
calling @code{R} which in turn calls @code{Q}). The cycle has been
tagged as @code{<c0>} for later references. The following section of the
report indicates that the use of a dynamically sized local object (its
size depends on an input argument) derives into a dynamic unbounded
frame. We then have the list of subprograms for which there is no
stack usage information.

Looking at the information about entry points we can see that the
computed stack requirements for the @code{main} entry point is 128
bytes. However, this result is not accurate (as indicated by the mark
@code{*}), and we see that the two problems that have been found is
the existence of cycles and unbounded frames.

The call chain information provides more detailed information about
the subprograms that are part of a cycle (@code{Q} and @code{R} in
this case), and the dynamic size of the frame for subprogram @code{Q}.

